1
00:00:01,230 --> 00:00:02,540
This is a banana.

2
00:00:02,720 --> 00:00:05,330
And roughly once every seventy-five minutes,

3
00:00:05,350 --> 00:00:06,790
it does something amazing.

4
00:00:07,130 --> 00:00:09,642
It shoots out a tiny speck of antimatter.

5
00:00:10,362 --> 00:00:11,127
A positron.

6
00:00:11,147 --> 00:00:12,093
It flies out,

7
00:00:12,113 --> 00:00:13,965
meets an ordinary electron,

8
00:00:13,985 --> 00:00:15,716
and both of them vanish.

9
00:00:15,736 --> 00:00:16,079
Gone.

10
00:00:16,099 --> 00:00:18,541
Turned into a flash of light.

11
00:00:19,451 --> 00:00:20,571
Here's the problem.

12
00:00:20,761 --> 00:00:22,644
Our best theory says the Big Bang

13
00:00:22,664 --> 00:00:25,281
made matter and antimatter in equal amounts.

14
00:00:25,591 --> 00:00:27,405
Every particle got a twin.

15
00:00:28,175 --> 00:00:29,926
And twins that meet, vanish.

16
00:00:29,946 --> 00:00:32,749
So the universe should have ended up empty.

17
00:00:32,769 --> 00:00:33,357
No stars.

18
00:00:33,377 --> 00:00:34,077
No planets.

19
00:00:34,097 --> 00:00:34,797
No bananas.

20
00:00:34,817 --> 00:00:35,663
No you.

21
00:00:36,683 --> 00:00:38,133
And yet, here you are.

22
00:00:38,523 --> 00:00:39,873
By the end of this video,

23
00:00:39,893 --> 00:00:42,063
you'll know what antimatter really is,

24
00:00:42,233 --> 00:00:46,303
how scientists hold something that destroys every box it touches,

25
00:00:46,493 --> 00:00:48,058
and the tiny clue that might

26
00:00:48,078 --> 00:00:50,281
explain why anything exists at all.

27
00:00:50,981 --> 00:00:53,739
So, why didn't the universe destroy itself?

28
00:01:00,509 --> 00:01:01,359
Chapter one.

29
00:01:01,379 --> 00:01:02,511
The mirror twin.

30
00:01:03,181 --> 00:01:05,591
Antimatter wasn't found in a lab first.

31
00:01:05,771 --> 00:01:07,550
It was found in an equation.

32
00:01:07,950 --> 00:01:09,250
In nineteen twenty-eight,

33
00:01:09,290 --> 00:01:11,440
a British physicist, Paul Dirac,

34
00:01:11,470 --> 00:01:13,300
wrote down an equation for the electron.

35
00:01:13,320 --> 00:01:13,900
It worked beautifully.

36
00:01:14,230 --> 00:01:15,570
But it also allowed something strange:

37
00:01:15,860 --> 00:01:18,070
a particle just like the electron,

38
00:01:18,270 --> 00:01:21,930
with the opposite charge.

39
00:01:22,400 --> 00:01:24,690
Four years later,

40
00:01:24,760 --> 00:01:28,800
in nineteen thirty-two,

41
00:01:29,140 --> 00:01:29,790
Carl Anderson was photographing cosmic rays in a cloud chamber.

42
00:01:29,810 --> 00:01:31,070
One track curved the wrong way.

43
00:01:31,300 --> 00:01:34,260
Same mass as an electron, but positive.

44
00:01:34,540 --> 00:01:35,713
The positron.

45
00:01:36,533 --> 00:01:39,523
Dirac won the Nobel Prize in nineteen thirty-three.

46
00:01:39,833 --> 00:01:42,493
Anderson won his in nineteen thirty-six.

47
00:01:42,943 --> 00:01:44,693
And it's not just electrons.

48
00:01:44,813 --> 00:01:46,883
Every particle has a mirror twin.

49
00:01:46,993 --> 00:01:49,233
Same mass, opposite charge.

50
00:01:49,423 --> 00:01:51,493
Protons have antiprotons.

51
00:01:51,763 --> 00:01:54,163
Hydrogen even has antihydrogen.

52
00:01:54,603 --> 00:01:55,428
Now, a myth:

53
00:01:55,448 --> 00:01:57,479
antimatter is science fiction.

54
00:01:57,499 --> 00:01:58,083
It isn't.

55
00:01:58,103 --> 00:01:59,229
Bananas make it,

56
00:01:59,249 --> 00:02:00,979
because of potassium-forty.

57
00:02:00,999 --> 00:02:03,814
And since your body needs potassium too,

58
00:02:03,834 --> 00:02:07,605
even you make a positron every once in a while.

59
00:02:08,455 --> 00:02:11,015
But if every particle was born with a twin,

60
00:02:11,125 --> 00:02:12,635
where did all the twins go?

61
00:02:14,325 --> 00:02:15,105
Chapter two.

62
00:02:15,125 --> 00:02:16,114
The flash.

63
00:02:16,814 --> 00:02:19,464
What happens when you shake hands with your mirror twin?

64
00:02:19,624 --> 00:02:21,003
You both disappear.

65
00:02:21,423 --> 00:02:24,623
When a particle meets its antiparticle, they annihilate.

66
00:02:24,943 --> 00:02:28,093
Their mass turns into pure energy, mostly light.

67
00:02:28,423 --> 00:02:31,513
That's Einstein's E equals m c squared,

68
00:02:31,533 --> 00:02:33,015
with nothing left over.

69
00:02:33,415 --> 00:02:35,495
And that energy is enormous.

70
00:02:35,865 --> 00:02:38,829
One gram of antimatter meeting one gram of matter

71
00:02:38,849 --> 00:02:41,510
would release about as much energy as a big

72
00:02:41,530 --> 00:02:44,131
power station makes in two whole days.

73
00:02:45,031 --> 00:02:46,329
So, myth number two:

74
00:02:46,349 --> 00:02:48,162
antimatter as a super fuel,

75
00:02:48,182 --> 00:02:49,307
or a super bomb.

76
00:02:49,327 --> 00:02:51,289
Here's the reality check.

77
00:02:51,689 --> 00:02:55,249
CERN, near Geneva,

78
00:02:55,689 --> 00:02:57,949
runs an antimatter factory.

79
00:02:58,099 --> 00:03:01,789
If it ran non-stop for a whole year,

80
00:03:01,809 --> 00:03:02,429
all its antiprotons together would light a one

81
00:03:02,449 --> 00:03:03,343
hundred watt bulb for about five seconds.

82
00:03:04,353 --> 00:03:06,193
Making antimatter is slow.

83
00:03:06,363 --> 00:03:07,943
Keeping it is even harder.

84
00:03:08,183 --> 00:03:10,593
The moment it touches the wall of its container,

85
00:03:10,613 --> 00:03:11,561
it's gone.

86
00:03:13,151 --> 00:03:14,061
Chapter three.

87
00:03:14,081 --> 00:03:15,366
How to hold nothing.

88
00:03:16,076 --> 00:03:17,951
How do you store something that destroys

89
00:03:17,971 --> 00:03:19,445
any box you put it in?

90
00:03:19,865 --> 00:03:21,425
You don't let it touch anything.

91
00:03:21,745 --> 00:03:25,515
Scientists build traps out of magnetic and electric fields,

92
00:03:25,595 --> 00:03:27,845
inside an almost perfect vacuum.

93
00:03:28,225 --> 00:03:31,094
The particles float in the middle, touching nothing.

94
00:03:31,504 --> 00:03:35,754
CERN's factory makes about four hundred million antiprotons an hour.

95
00:03:36,164 --> 00:03:39,287
The experiments manage to catch about ten percent.

96
00:03:39,697 --> 00:03:41,737
One experiment, called ALPHA,

97
00:03:41,807 --> 00:03:43,667
builds whole anti-atoms.

98
00:03:44,067 --> 00:03:47,307
Up to three thousand antihydrogen atoms an hour,

99
00:03:47,397 --> 00:03:49,465
kept for up to one hundred hours.

100
00:03:49,865 --> 00:03:51,075
In twenty twenty-three,

101
00:03:51,095 --> 00:03:53,115
ALPHA asked a simple question.

102
00:03:53,475 --> 00:03:55,715
Does antimatter fall down, or up?

103
00:03:55,785 --> 00:03:57,255
They let the atoms go.

104
00:03:57,505 --> 00:03:58,425
They fell down,

105
00:03:58,445 --> 00:04:00,047
just like normal matter.

106
00:04:00,957 --> 00:04:03,777
This year, antimatter even went on a road trip.

107
00:04:04,187 --> 00:04:05,677
On the twenty-fourth of March,

108
00:04:05,707 --> 00:04:08,997
ninety-two antiprotons rode around CERN on a truck,

109
00:04:09,167 --> 00:04:11,767
inside a portable trap called BASE-STEP.

110
00:04:12,157 --> 00:04:14,977
The plan is to drive them to quieter labs,

111
00:04:15,077 --> 00:04:17,637
away from the factory's magnetic noise.

112
00:04:18,347 --> 00:04:23,307
Then, in October,

113
00:04:23,327 --> 00:04:24,222
a team at Northwestern University in the

114
00:04:24,242 --> 00:04:25,617
United States reported something even more delicate.

115
00:04:26,097 --> 00:04:27,587
A single positron,

116
00:04:27,607 --> 00:04:28,657
held for months,

117
00:04:28,817 --> 00:04:32,039
at about a third of a degree above absolute zero.

118
00:04:32,849 --> 00:04:33,969
Why all this effort,

119
00:04:33,989 --> 00:04:35,529
for one tiny particle?

120
00:04:35,659 --> 00:04:37,561
Because they're hunting for a difference.

121
00:04:38,731 --> 00:04:39,661
Chapter four.

122
00:04:39,681 --> 00:04:40,902
One in a billion.

123
00:04:41,582 --> 00:04:44,277
Everything you have ever seen is a leftover.

124
00:04:44,747 --> 00:04:47,258
Physicists can compare how much matter survived

125
00:04:47,278 --> 00:04:49,507
with how much light was left behind.

126
00:04:49,817 --> 00:04:51,403
And the answer is tiny.

127
00:04:51,833 --> 00:04:53,762
For every billion pairs of matter and

128
00:04:53,782 --> 00:04:55,893
antimatter that wiped each other out,

129
00:04:56,023 --> 00:04:58,983
about one extra particle of matter survived.

130
00:04:59,283 --> 00:05:00,194
Just one.

131
00:05:01,294 --> 00:05:03,727
Every star, every planet, every person,

132
00:05:03,747 --> 00:05:05,666
is made from those survivors.

133
00:05:05,686 --> 00:05:07,231
One in a billion.

134
00:05:08,191 --> 00:05:10,991
And light from that early universe is still here.

135
00:05:11,391 --> 00:05:14,651
About four hundred and eleven particles of ancient light,

136
00:05:14,771 --> 00:05:17,589
in every cubic centimetre of space around you.

137
00:05:17,989 --> 00:05:19,520
Now, myth number three.

138
00:05:19,540 --> 00:05:24,115
Maybe the missing antimatter is just hiding somewhere, in anti-stars,

139
00:05:24,135 --> 00:05:25,526
or anti-galaxies.

140
00:05:25,916 --> 00:05:28,246
If it were,

141
00:05:28,266 --> 00:05:30,686
we'd see bright flashes where anti-regions touch normal ones.

142
00:05:31,016 --> 00:05:32,966
No such zones have been found.

143
00:05:33,326 --> 00:05:35,399
Physicists now think no part of the

144
00:05:35,419 --> 00:05:38,114
observable universe is made of antimatter.

145
00:05:38,934 --> 00:05:41,104
So the universe didn't hide its antimatter.

146
00:05:41,124 --> 00:05:42,064
It lost it.

147
00:05:42,254 --> 00:05:45,584
Which means the laws of physics must slightly favour matter.

148
00:05:45,884 --> 00:05:46,750
But how?

149
00:05:48,330 --> 00:05:49,300
Chapter five.

150
00:05:49,320 --> 00:05:50,705
The rule breakers.

151
00:05:51,385 --> 00:05:53,025
In nineteen sixty-seven,

152
00:05:53,225 --> 00:05:55,251
a Soviet physicist wrote down the

153
00:05:55,271 --> 00:05:57,535
recipe for a universe like ours.

154
00:05:57,995 --> 00:06:00,075
His name was Andrei Sakharov.

155
00:06:00,315 --> 00:06:02,273
He said three things must be true.

156
00:06:02,673 --> 00:06:02,934
One:

157
00:06:02,954 --> 00:06:06,923
nature needs a way to make more particles than antiparticles.

158
00:06:06,943 --> 00:06:10,968
Two: matter and antimatter must follow slightly different rules.

159
00:06:10,988 --> 00:06:13,834
Three: it all had to happen out of balance,

160
00:06:13,854 --> 00:06:14,620
in the hot,

161
00:06:14,640 --> 00:06:16,269
fast early universe.

162
00:06:17,209 --> 00:06:18,789
That second rule has a name:

163
00:06:18,909 --> 00:06:20,529
C P violation.

164
00:06:20,729 --> 00:06:22,289
And we have actually seen it.

165
00:06:22,679 --> 00:06:24,129
In nineteen sixty-four,

166
00:06:24,149 --> 00:06:27,609
James Cronin and Val Fitch found the first tiny difference,

167
00:06:27,649 --> 00:06:29,591
in particles called mesons.

168
00:06:29,991 --> 00:06:34,331
Then, in twenty twenty-five,

169
00:06:34,351 --> 00:06:35,489
the LHCb experiment at CERN saw it

170
00:06:35,509 --> 00:06:36,751
for the first time in baryons,

171
00:06:36,911 --> 00:06:39,451
the family of protons and neutrons.

172
00:06:39,911 --> 00:06:41,661
A particle called the beauty lambda

173
00:06:41,681 --> 00:06:43,911
decayed differently from its antiparticle,

174
00:06:44,071 --> 00:06:46,041
by about two and a half percent.

175
00:06:46,441 --> 00:06:49,341
It took more than eighty thousand decays to be sure.

176
00:06:49,701 --> 00:06:52,570
The result was published in Nature in July.

177
00:06:52,970 --> 00:06:54,310
But here's the twist.

178
00:06:54,640 --> 00:06:56,911
The amount of difference our best theory

179
00:06:56,931 --> 00:06:59,201
predicts is many orders of magnitude too

180
00:06:59,221 --> 00:07:01,489
small to explain why we are here.

181
00:07:02,379 --> 00:07:04,059
The rule breaking is real.

182
00:07:04,149 --> 00:07:05,889
There just isn't nearly enough of it.

183
00:07:05,959 --> 00:07:07,345
Something is missing.

184
00:07:09,025 --> 00:07:09,995
Chapter six.

185
00:07:10,015 --> 00:07:11,719
The hunt for the missing piece.

186
00:07:12,399 --> 00:07:13,849
If the answer exists,

187
00:07:13,949 --> 00:07:15,569
it's hiding in a difference smaller

188
00:07:15,589 --> 00:07:17,462
than anything we've ever measured.

189
00:07:17,882 --> 00:07:20,032
One suspect is the neutrino,

190
00:07:20,142 --> 00:07:23,172
a ghostly particle that barely touches anything.

191
00:07:23,592 --> 00:07:26,722
A matter-antimatter difference in neutrinos may exist,

192
00:07:26,842 --> 00:07:28,514
but it hasn't been measured yet.

193
00:07:28,914 --> 00:07:31,694
Another way is to compare the twins directly,

194
00:07:31,844 --> 00:07:34,224
as precisely as humanly possible.

195
00:07:34,624 --> 00:07:37,923
That's what the single positron at Northwestern is for.

196
00:07:38,343 --> 00:07:41,803
The team wants to measure how magnetic the positron is,

197
00:07:41,943 --> 00:07:43,873
and compare it with the electron.

198
00:07:44,323 --> 00:07:46,314
The electron has already been measured

199
00:07:46,334 --> 00:07:48,207
to one part in ten trillion.

200
00:07:48,617 --> 00:07:50,657
If the twins are perfect mirrors,

201
00:07:50,687 --> 00:07:52,597
the numbers will match exactly.

202
00:07:53,057 --> 00:07:55,207
If they don't, Gerald Gabrielse,

203
00:07:55,227 --> 00:07:56,417
who leads the lab,

204
00:07:56,457 --> 00:07:58,344
says it would be the biggest discovery

205
00:07:58,364 --> 00:08:00,241
in physics in the last century.

206
00:08:01,251 --> 00:08:03,974
So far, every test says the same thing.

207
00:08:03,994 --> 00:08:05,107
The twins match.

208
00:08:05,127 --> 00:08:05,822
Same mass.

209
00:08:05,842 --> 00:08:06,538
Same fall.

210
00:08:06,558 --> 00:08:09,653
Which makes our existence even stranger.

211
00:08:10,553 --> 00:08:13,311
So, why didn't the universe destroy itself?

212
00:08:13,731 --> 00:08:15,141
The honest answer is:

213
00:08:15,161 --> 00:08:16,191
we don't know yet.

214
00:08:16,291 --> 00:08:18,001
But we know how close it came.

215
00:08:18,251 --> 00:08:21,590
For every billion particles that vanished, one survived.

216
00:08:22,600 --> 00:08:24,480
You are made of those survivors.

217
00:08:24,720 --> 00:08:26,274
Every atom in you came through

218
00:08:26,294 --> 00:08:28,421
the biggest disappearance in history.

219
00:08:29,521 --> 00:08:32,181
So the next time you peel a banana, remember.

220
00:08:32,551 --> 00:08:33,791
Somewhere inside it,

221
00:08:33,811 --> 00:08:35,503
a tiny flash is replaying the

222
00:08:35,523 --> 00:08:37,391
oldest fight in the universe.

223
00:08:37,581 --> 00:08:39,191
And matter is still winning.

224
00:08:40,411 --> 00:08:41,641
If you enjoyed this,

225
00:08:41,661 --> 00:08:44,051
like the video and subscribe to Whyverse.

226
00:08:44,381 --> 00:08:45,791
And for shorter stories,

227
00:08:45,901 --> 00:08:48,105
find us on Instagram and TikTok.

